---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Byline from '../../components/Byline.astro';
import { SR, RATES, ADS, SPONSORS, PITCHES } from '../../data/sr-stats';
import { articleSchema, faqSchema } from '../../lib/article';

const PATH = '/blog/find-sponsorship-opportunities-guide/';
const PUBLISHED = '2026-01-28';
const MODIFIED = '2026-10-02';
const THIRD_PARTY_READ = '2 October 2026';

const n = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-US');
const usd = (x: number) => `$${x.toLocaleString('en-US')}`;
const [r1, r2, r3, r4, r5] = RATES.rows;
const seen = PITCHES.evidence.seen;
const none = PITCHES.evidence.none;

const title = 'How to Find Sponsorship Opportunities (By Channel, 2026)';
const description =
  "Find sponsorship opportunities by joining your platform's marketplace and pitching brands that already sponsor creators like you. Steps by creator type.";
const h1 = 'How to Find Sponsorship Opportunities in 2026';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Where to find sponsorship opportunities for high-viewership content?',
    a: `Larger audiences qualify for more programs. As of ${THIRD_PARTY_READ}, TikTok One asks for 10,000 followers, Libsyn Ads takes podcasts with 20,000 downloads per episode, and YouTube Creator Partnerships shows eligible Partner Program channels to brands. For newsletters, a list twice the size lists its main sponsor slot about ${RATES.doublingLift} higher, in Sponsor Radar's read of ${RATES.n} newsletters on ${RATES.read}.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I find sponsorship opportunities that fit my brand values and target audience?',
    a: `Start from your audience, then pick brands that already sponsor creators they follow. Read the sponsor slots of channels, podcasts and newsletters like yours, and keep the brands whose product you would use. Brands with a newsletter ad on record replied to Sponsor Radar's pitches at ${seen.rate} (${n(seen.replies)} of ${n(seen.sent)}), against ${none.rate} (${n(none.replies)} of ${n(none.sent)}) for brands with none, from ${PITCHES.window}.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a tool that matches brands with sponsorship opportunities based on audience demographics and goals?',
    a: `Yes, each channel has its own. YouTube builds a media kit from your channel insights and says sharing them helps brands pick creators for their campaign goals. Instagram's creator marketplace lets brands see your content and insights such as average reach and followers. Passionfroot's ad network sends sponsorship requests to creators. Sponsor Radar matches a newsletter to brands that sponsored similar newsletters, not by reader demographics.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What kinds of marketplaces let creators receive and accept sponsored offers without doing outreach?',
    a: `Platform programs where brands search for creators and send offers: YouTube Creator Partnerships, Instagram's creator marketplace, TikTok One, the Twitch Sponsorship Portal, Libsyn Ads for podcasts, and Collabstr and Passionfroot across several channels. Each has entry rules, listed in the table above, and you accept or decline every offer. Read ${THIRD_PARTY_READ}.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Which platforms show which creators competitors sponsor across social media?',
    a: `Meta's Ad Library has a searchable database of active public branded content on Facebook and Instagram with a paid partnership label. YouTube adds a paid promotion label to sponsored videos, and sponsorradar.com, a YouTube sponsorship database unrelated to Sponsor Radar, lists channels with their sponsors. For newsletters, Sponsor Radar's free sponsors list and SponsorGap show who sponsors which newsletter. Read ${THIRD_PARTY_READ}.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How many followers or subscribers do you need to get sponsored?',
    a: `There is no single number. As of ${THIRD_PARTY_READ}, TikTok One asks for 10,000 followers, Libsyn Ads for 20,000 podcast downloads per episode, and YouTube Creator Partnerships for Partner Program membership. Small newsletters sell ads too: ${ADS.bySize[0].share} of those ${ADS.bySize[0].size.toLowerCase()} subscribers ran a paid sponsor ad in the 90 days to ${ADS.read}, in Sponsor Radar's data.`,
  },
];

const schema = [
  articleSchema({ headline: title, description, path: PATH, published: PUBLISHED, modified: MODIFIED }),
  faqSchema(faq),
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={schema} articleDate={PUBLISHED} articleModified={MODIFIED}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12 md:py-20">
    <header>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900">{h1}</h1>
      <div class="mt-3"><Byline published={PUBLISHED} modified={MODIFIED} /></div>
    </header>
    <div class="mt-8 prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p><strong>You find sponsorship opportunities two ways: join the marketplace or platform program for your channel so brands send you offers, and pitch brands that already sponsor creators like you. Run both. The marketplace brings offers with little work, and pitching lets you choose the brand and set the price.</strong></p>

      <p>This guide covers where to launch on each channel, how to see who your competitors' sponsors are, what to do for YouTube, podcasts, newsletters, Instagram, TikTok, Twitch and events, and what sponsorships pay. Every number names its source, starting with this one: {ADS.share} of {ADS.n.toLocaleString('en-US')} newsletters that published 5 or more issues ran a paid sponsor ad in the 90 days to {ADS.read} (Sponsor Radar's data), so most newsletters run no paid ads at all. Facts about other companies' programs were read on {THIRD_PARTY_READ} and can change.</p>

      <h2 id="where-to-launch">Where can you launch creator sponsorships today?</h2>

      <p>Launch on the marketplace or platform program for your channel first, then add direct outreach. A marketplace shows brands your audience and lets them contact you, so you wait for offers. Outreach means you pick the brands and write to them, so you choose the sponsor and set the price.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Marketplace:</strong> brands come to you, and offers arrive in your inbox or a platform tab. Some platforms keep a cut. Libsyn Ads keeps 30% of podcast ad revenue and pays the podcaster 70% (<a href="https://advertising.libsyn.com/monetize-your-podcast" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Libsyn Ads</a>, read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}).</li>
        <li><strong>Outreach:</strong> you choose the brand, the offer and the price, and no platform takes a cut. It is slower. Sponsor Radar's automated outreach sent {n(PITCHES.sent)} first pitches to brands for {PITCHES.newsletters} newsletters, {PITCHES.window}, and drew {PITCHES.yeses} positive first answers, about {PITCHES.pitchesPerYes} pitches per yes. Those are one short email per brand, not hand-written pitches, so a pitch you target yourself may do better or worse.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Our <a href="/best-sponsorship-platforms/">comparison of 15 sponsorship platforms</a> ranks each one on how much of the selling it does for you, its published fees and who can join. The next sections show where offers arrive on each channel and how to run the outreach side.</p>

      <h2>Which marketplaces let you accept sponsored offers without outreach?</h2>

      <p>A sponsorship marketplace is a program where brands browse creators and send offers, and you accept or decline each one. YouTube, Instagram, TikTok, Twitch and podcasts each have one, and each has its own entry rules.</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Channel</th>
              <th>Where offers arrive</th>
              <th>Who can join</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>YouTube</td>
              <td>The Creator Partnerships tab in YouTube Studio. You answer Interested or Not interested.</td>
              <td>YouTube Partner Program members aged 18 or older, with no active Community Guidelines strikes, in a listed country that includes the US.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Instagram</td>
              <td>A Partnership messages folder, after you join the creator marketplace from your professional dashboard.</td>
              <td>A professional account on Instagram.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>TikTok</td>
              <td>TikTok One.</td>
              <td>Aged 18 or older, an account in good standing, 10,000 followers (the requirement can differ by region), 1,000 post views and 3 posts in the last 30 days.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Twitch</td>
              <td>The Sponsorship Portal: Exclusive Campaigns (brands that chose you), Open-Invite Campaigns (first come, first served) and Third-Party Campaigns.</td>
              <td>Partners, and Affiliates who complete Creator Sponsorship Certification.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Podcasts</td>
              <td>The Libsyn Ads marketplace. Advertisers order ad buys and you approve or deny each one.</td>
              <td>Podcasts with at least 20,000 downloads per episode.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Several channels</td>
              <td>Collabstr: brands hire you from your profile, or you apply to campaigns they post.</td>
              <td>Free to start.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Several channels</td>
              <td>Passionfroot: sponsorship requests from its ad network arrive in your inbox, and you can apply to its Live Campaigns.</td>
              <td>Free to start.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Sources, all read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}: <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/9385307" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube Help, Get started with YouTube Creator Partnerships</a>; <a href="https://www.facebook.com/business/help/3109798062682672" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Meta Business Help Center, Instagram's creator marketplace</a>; <a href="https://support.tiktok.com/en/business-and-creator/tiktok-one" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TikTok Support, who is eligible to join TikTok One</a>; <a href="https://help.twitch.tv/s/article/sponsorship-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Twitch Help, Sponsorship Portal Overview</a>; <a href="https://advertising.libsyn.com/monetize-your-podcast" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Libsyn Ads, monetize your podcast</a>; <a href="https://collabstr.com/creator" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Collabstr for creators</a>; <a href="https://www.passionfroot.me/creators" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Passionfroot for creators</a>.</p>

      <p>An offer from a platform is a starting point. YouTube's help page says the amount on an inquiry comes from the advertiser and is not a final offer, and that if you answer Interested you negotiate and sign with the brand directly.</p>

      <h2>Which tools match brands to creators by audience and goals?</h2>

      <p>Each channel has its own matcher, and most of them work from the audience data you share. YouTube builds a media kit from your channel insights, and its help page says turning on insights sharing improves your visibility in creator search and helps brands pick creators for their campaign goals. On Instagram, brands that find you in the creator marketplace can see your content and insights such as average reach and follower count. Passionfroot's ad network sends sponsorship requests to creators, and its Live Campaigns board lets you apply to brands looking for your profile.</p>

      <p>For newsletters, Sponsor Radar matches on the niche, not on demographics. It lists the brands that sponsored newsletters similar to yours ({n(SPONSORS.brands)} brands seen sponsoring {n(SPONSORS.newsletters)} newsletters, {SPONSORS.read}) and emails them for you. Any matcher is only as good as the numbers you give it, so build a <a href="/blog/sponsorship-media-kit-guide/">media kit with accurate audience data</a> first, or start with the <a href="/tools/media-kit-builder/">media kit builder</a>.</p>

      <h2>How do you see which creators a competitor sponsors?</h2>

      <p>On some channels a free public record shows who sponsors whom, and on others you need a database. Check the record for each channel you sell on:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Facebook and Instagram:</strong> Meta's Ad Library has a searchable database of all active, public branded content with a paid partnership label (<a href="https://transparency.meta.com/researchtools/ad-library-tools/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Meta Transparency Center</a>, read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}).</li>
        <li><strong>YouTube:</strong> creators who feature a sponsor select the paid promotion button, which adds a disclosure label at the start of the video (<a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/154235" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube Help</a>, read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}), so a competitor's recent videos show its sponsors. <a href="https://sponsorradar.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">sponsorradar.com</a>, a different company from Sponsor Radar, is a YouTube sponsorship database that lists channels with their sponsors and has a free plan (its homepage, read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}).</li>
        <li><strong>Newsletters:</strong> Sponsor Radar's <a href={SR.sponsorsUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">public newsletter sponsors list</a> shows the brands seen sponsoring newsletters, and <a href="https://sponsorgap.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SponsorGap</a> lists competitor alerts on its $89 a month plan (its homepage, read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}).</li>
        <li><strong>Podcasts:</strong> listen to the ad reads in recent episodes of shows like yours and write down every brand.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Put the brands in one sheet with the creator, the format and the last date you saw them. A brand that appears on several creators is running a program, and that is the one to pitch first. The full method is in <a href="/blog/find-brands-that-sponsor/">how to find brands that already sponsor creators like you</a>.</p>

      <h2>How do you find sponsors for your channel type?</h2>

      <p>Each channel has its own fastest route. Use the one that matches where your audience is.</p>

      <h3>YouTube</h3>

      <p>YouTube brings sponsors to eligible channels through the Creator Partnerships tab, and you can pitch brands in parallel. As of {THIRD_PARTY_READ}, YouTube's help page says Partner Program channels aged 18 or older, with no active strikes, in a listed country are discoverable to brands. You set desired rates for long-form videos and Shorts, and answering Interested shares your contact details so you can negotiate with the brand. Turn on channel insights sharing to improve your visibility in creator search. If your channel is not eligible yet, watch videos in your niche for the paid promotion label, list the brands and <a href="/blog/cold-email-sponsorship-pitch/">pitch them by email</a>.</p>

      <h3>Podcasts</h3>

      <p>Podcast sponsors come from ad marketplaces and from direct pitches. Libsyn Ads accepts podcasts with at least 20,000 downloads per episode, lets you approve or deny each ad buy, and keeps 30% of ad revenue (<a href="https://advertising.libsyn.com/monetize-your-podcast" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Libsyn Ads</a>, read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}). Below that size, sell direct. Listen to the ad reads on shows like yours, list the brands, and pitch them with your downloads per episode and a price per spot. Podcast prices are quoted per 1,000 downloads, as the pricing section below shows. Our <a href="/blog/how-to-get-podcast-sponsors/">study of the top 200 podcasts</a> lists the brands that sponsor the most shows right now.</p>

      <h3>Newsletters</h3>

      <p>Newsletter sponsors are the easiest to research because the ads are printed in the issues. Sponsor Radar's data covers {n(ADS.n)} newsletters with five or more issues in the 90 days to {ADS.read}. {ADS.share} of them ran a paid sponsor ad, from {ADS.bySize[0].share} of those with {ADS.bySize[0].size.toLowerCase()} subscribers to {ADS.bySize[4].share} of those with {ADS.bySize[4].size.toLowerCase()}. Sponsor Radar's <a href={SR.sponsorsUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">public sponsors list</a> shows {n(SPONSORS.brands)} brands seen sponsoring {n(SPONSORS.newsletters)} newsletters as of {SPONSORS.read}, mostly read from published issues and the rest from sponsor pages and articles. Sponsor Radar publishes this site. It emails brands for newsletter owners, with $20 in free credits (200 emails), then 10¢ per email sent.</p>

      <h3>Instagram and TikTok</h3>

      <p>Join the marketplace on each app and keep pitching in parallel. On Instagram, open your professional dashboard, tap Branded content, then Join creator marketplace. Brands can then see your content and insights and message you in a Partnership messages folder (Meta Business Help Center, read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}). TikTok One asks for 10,000 followers, 1,000 post views and 3 posts in the last 30 days, and the follower requirement can differ by region (TikTok Support, read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}). Below that, set up a profile on Collabstr, where brands hire you or you apply to campaigns, and pitch brands you already use.</p>

      <h3>Twitch and streamers</h3>

      <p>Twitch runs its own sponsorship portal for Affiliates and Partners. Affiliates must complete Creator Sponsorship Certification, a self-paced course on Creator Camp, to be eligible for campaign invitations. The portal's Browse page shows three types of campaign: Exclusive Campaigns from brands that chose you, Open-Invite Campaigns on a first-come, first-served basis, and Third-Party Campaigns sourced from partners (<a href="https://help.twitch.tv/s/article/sponsorship-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Twitch Help</a>, read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}). Outside the portal, list the brands that sponsor streamers in your category and pitch them with a short clip reel and your average live viewers.</p>

      <h3>Events</h3>

      <p>Event sponsors are the brands that already sponsored comparable events. Read the sponsor pages of events like yours, note which brands appear and at what level, and pitch their marketing or partnerships contacts. Send a prospectus that lists your attendees, your sponsorship levels and what each level includes, so a brand can say yes to one specific package. Our guide to <a href="/blog/sponsorship-packages/">sponsorship packages and levels</a> shows how to build the tiers, and <a href="/blog/media-sponsorship/">media sponsorship</a> explains how to value placements in your own channels.</p>

      <h2>How do you pitch brands directly?</h2>

      <p>Pitch brands that already sponsor creators like you, with a short email that names one audience number and one specific offer. They reply more. In Sponsor Radar's pitches from {PITCHES.window}, brands with a newsletter ad on record replied at {seen.rate} ({n(seen.replies)} of {n(seen.sent)}), against {none.rate} ({n(none.replies)} of {n(none.sent)}) for brands with none. That compares reply rates only, because the yes counts are too small to compare, and it is two groups of brands, not a controlled test. The pitches were Sponsor Radar's automated outreach for newsletters, read {PITCHES.read}.</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Build the brand list.</strong> Use the competitor sheet above and the methods in <a href="/blog/find-brands-that-sponsor/">how to find brands that sponsor creators</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Find the person who buys.</strong> Look for a partnerships, creator or growth lead, not a generic info@ address. Verify the email before you send.</li>
        <li><strong>Send a short pitch.</strong> Name one audience number, reference the brand's current marketing and propose one placement. Templates are in the <a href="/blog/cold-email-sponsorship-pitch/">sponsorship pitch email guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Follow up.</strong> A brand that stays silent may answer a second email. See <a href="/blog/sponsorship-follow-up-email/">sponsorship follow-up email templates</a> for wording and timing.</li>
        <li><strong>Count your pitches.</strong> <a href="/blog/how-many-brands-to-pitch/">How many brands to pitch</a> shows the numbers from Sponsor Radar's outreach and how to plan your own.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What do sponsorships pay?</h2>

      <p>A newsletter's main sponsor slot lists at a median {usd(r1.median)} {r1.size.toLowerCase()} subscribers and {usd(r5.median)} at {r5.size.toLowerCase()}. The main slot is the top sponsor placement in one issue.</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Subscribers</th>
              <th>Newsletters read</th>
              <th>Median main slot</th>
              <th>Middle half of newsletters</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {RATES.rows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{r.size}</td>
                <td>{r.n}</td>
                <td><strong>{usd(r.median)}</strong></td>
                <td>{usd(r.p25)} to {usd(r.p75)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Source: Sponsor Radar's read of {RATES.n} newsletters' advertise pages, {RATES.read}. These are list prices that newsletters print, not what brands finally pay. Method and the full table: <a href={SR.ratesUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">newsletter sponsorship rates by list size</a>.</p>

      <p>A list twice the size lists its main slot about {RATES.doublingLift} higher, so bigger newsletters charge less per subscriber. For podcasts, Libsyn Ads lists baked-in host-read ads at $24 to $26 per 1,000 downloads for a 60-second spot and $18 to $22 for a 30-second spot (<a href="https://advertising.libsyn.com/podcast-advertising-ultimate-guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Libsyn Ads guide</a>, read {THIRD_PARTY_READ}). For example, at $25 CPM, a show with 10,000 downloads per episode would list a 60-second spot at 10,000 ÷ 1,000 × $25 = $250. Those round numbers are hypothetical.</p>

      <p>For YouTube, Instagram and TikTok ranges from Descript, Sprout Social and impact.com, each dated, and for the math behind a CPM, use the <a href="/blog/how-much-to-charge-for-sponsorship/">sponsorship pricing guide</a> and the <a href="/tools/sponsorship-rate-calculator/">sponsorship rate calculator</a>.</p>

      <h2>What mistakes lose sponsorship deals?</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pitching too broad.</strong> "I'd love to work with your brand" is not a pitch. Propose a specific placement, such as a 60-second integration in your next video on a topic the brand sells into.</li>
        <li><strong>No media kit.</strong> When a brand shows interest, it needs your audience, your past results and your rates in one place. Have a <a href="/blog/sponsorship-media-kit-guide/">media kit</a> ready to send.</li>
        <li><strong>Pricing from guesswork.</strong> Price from your audience and a CPM for your format, not from a number that feels right.</li>
        <li><strong>Sending one email.</strong> Brands have busy inboxes and budget cycles. Follow up politely before you write a brand off.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions about finding sponsorship opportunities</h2>

      {faq.map((f) => (
        <Fragment>
          <h3>{f.q}</h3>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </Fragment>
      ))}

      <h2>Where should you start this week?</h2>

      <p>Join the marketplace for your channel from the table above, then build a list of brands that already sponsor creators like you and send the first pitches. If you send a newsletter, <a href={SR.cta('find-sponsorship-opportunities-guide')}>Sponsor Radar</a> shows the brands that sponsored similar newsletters and the person at each one who buys the ads. It covers newsletters only, not YouTube, podcasts or events.</p>

    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
